# findings-two-node.env — what the TWO-NODE sandbox measured, bound to its subject.
#
# SEPARATE FROM `findings.env` ON PURPOSE. That file records what Calico's `first-found`
# autodetection does, and these nodes are explicitly TOLD which interface to use
# (`cidr=10.77.0.0/24`) because both of their slirp NICs hold the identical address
# 10.0.2.15. Merging the two would be the substitution this repo keeps getting caught by:
# same lab, same Calico, different question.

# ── the subject ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NCS2_CALICO_VERSION=v3.29.3
NCS2_MICROK8S_VERSION=v1.35.6
NCS2_TOPOLOGY=two-nodes-one-private-qemu-socket-wire
NCS2_MEASURED_ON=2026-08-08

# ── the wire ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two VMs joined by QEMU's `socket` netdev on 127.0.0.1 — a private L2 segment between
# exactly those two processes. No bridge, no tap, no host interface, no root. The host's own
# Calico binding was `local 192.168.1.106 dev enx00051b8eb138` before, during and after.
#
# This is the capability the lab was missing rather than a fact about Calico: `network_mode
# = tap|bridge` are the only other ways two of this repo's VMs can share a network, and both
# are root-gated and both change the HOST's networking — disqualifying for a lab whose whole
# premise is that breaking a CNI costs nothing.
NCS2_PRIVATE_WIRE_WORKS=confirmed
NCS2_WIRE_NEEDS_ROOT=no
NCS2_HOST_NETWORKING_TOUCHED=no

# ── the cluster, and the three things that had to be fixed to get one ───────────────────
#
# All three are the same shape, and it is this repo's bug class #1: a record that outlives
# the address it describes. None of them failed at the step that caused them.

# 1. EVERY SLIRP GUEST IS 10.0.2.15. The leader advertised that as its API server endpoint,
#    so the worker's apiserver-proxy dialled *itself* — while `microk8s join` printed
#    "Successfully joined the cluster." kubelet then looped on `Unable to register node …
#    EOF` and the node never appeared. A false success on top of an address that means a
#    different machine on every guest. Fixed with `--advertise-address` BEFORE the token is
#    minted, because the token carries the endpoint.
NCS2_SLIRP_SHARED_ADDRESS_BREAKS_JOIN=confirmed

# 2. THE KUBELET SERVING CERT IS A SEPARATE CERTIFICATE from the API server's, with its own
#    SAN list built at install time. Moving `--node-ip` does not reissue it: it stays valid
#    and simply stops describing its subject. Nothing fails at join, at `get nodes`, or at
#    any readiness check — it fails at the first `kubectl exec`, which is the chaos harness's
#    ONLY dataplane observable. A two-node matrix would have graded every row STRANDED and
#    blamed the CNI.
#
#    `refresh-certs --cert server.crt` does NOT cover it — that was tried, printed a
#    confident success, and changed nothing that mattered. `server.crt` gained the address;
#    `kubelet.crt` did not.
NCS2_KUBELET_CERT_IS_SEPARATE_FROM_APISERVER_CERT=confirmed

# 3. ...and the marker that said so was a MECHANISM claim: it printed because a command
#    exited 0, not because a SAN had changed. The reissue now reads the SAN back out of the
#    certificate on disk and refuses if it is not there.
NCS2_ASSERT_THE_SAN_NOT_THE_EXIT_STATUS=confirmed

# ── what a second node makes measurable, and one node could not ─────────────────────────
#
# Two calico-node pods, two DISTINCT VXLAN tunnel addresses (10.1.76.0 and 10.1.149.0), two
# distinct IPAM blocks, and pod-to-pod traffic that can only get there through the tunnel:
#
#   p1 10.1.76.5 (on calico-n1)  ->  p2 10.1.149.1 (on calico-n2)
#   3 packets transmitted, 3 received, 0% packet loss, avg 0.459 ms
#
# The one-node matrix grades its `vxlan-deleted` row on whether Calico REBUILDS the device,
# and says in the open that this is because the tunnel carries no traffic with no peers. That
# rung was an UNKNOWN wearing a DEGRADED. Here the tunnel carries a measurable dataplane.
NCS2_CROSS_NODE_DATAPLANE=confirmed
NCS2_TUNNEL_ADDRS_DISTINCT=confirmed

# ── the CROSS-NODE CHAOS ROWS, measured 2026-08-15 ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both rows the one-node matrix could only grade on a proxy, asked with a peer. Injected by
# `cross-node-chaos.sh`, graded by `tests/test-cross-node-chaos.sh` on CLAUDE.md's ladder.
NCS2_CROSS_NODE_CHAOS_ROWS=measured-2026-08-15
NCS2_XROW_TUNNEL_DELETED_UNDER_PEER=DEGRADED
NCS2_XROW_PEER_ADDRESS_MOVED=DEGRADED

# ⚠️ THE DURATIONS BELOW ARE RANGES, AND THAT IS THE FINDING RATHER THAN AN APOLOGY. Calico
#    re-detects on a timer, so where a fault lands in that cycle decides how long the outage
#    lasts: seven runs on the same pair produced 1-9 s for one row and 17-55 s for the other.
#    Recording the last sample as "the number" would have been a cached fact — the first draft
#    of this file did exactly that with `55`, from two runs that happened to agree.
#    What is stable, and is what the tests assert, is the RUNG.
NCS2_XMEASURED_RUNS=7

# ① THE TUNNEL, DELETED WHILE IT WAS CARRYING TRAFFIC. The one-node row can only ask whether
#    Calico rebuilds the device. Here the packets stopped and came back on their own, and the
#    interesting field is the one a single node has no reason to print: with vxlan.calico gone,
#    the leader's route to the peer's pods fell through to `enp0s3` — the SLIRP NIC — so the
#    overlay's traffic was handed to a NAT gateway that has never heard of 10.1.149.0/26.
#    A dead overlay does not fail closed; it fails to the default route.
NCS2_XTUNNEL_OUTAGE_SECONDS_RANGE=1-9
NCS2_XTUNNEL_PACKETS_LOST_RANGE=1-6
NCS2_XTUNNEL_FALLS_THROUGH_TO_DEFAULT_ROUTE=confirmed

# ② F.6 WITH A WITNESS. Node 2's address moved 10.77.0.12 -> 10.77.0.22 on the wire while
#    node 1's pod was pinging node 2's pod. Cross-node traffic died AT ONCE and came back by
#    itself once Calico re-detected.
#
#    THE PROPERTY THE INCIDENT WAS ACTUALLY MADE OF, and it needs no range because it did not
#    vary: IN EVERY SAMPLE OF EVERY RUN THAT SAW THE DATAPLANE DOWN, EVERY NODE READ `Ready`.
#    The cluster never once admitted the outage, and node 2's Calico annotation went on naming
#    an address that had ceased to exist for the whole of it. That is the shape of F.6 on the
#    host: not a node that broke, but an outage that looked like a healthy cluster.
NCS2_XADDR_OUTAGE_SECONDS_RANGE=17-55
NCS2_XADDR_PACKETS_LOST_RANGE=12-50
NCS2_XADDR_CLUSTER_EVER_ADMITTED_THE_OUTAGE=no

# ②b …AND THE RUNG IS DECIDED BY PACKETS LOST, NOT BY A POST-INJECTION SAMPLE. That sample
#    races the CNI's recovery — the tunnel rebuild has been measured at 1 s against a probe
#    that takes 3-4 s — and losing the race would score ABSORBED, reading as "the CNI shrugged
#    off having its overlay deleted". A 1-per-second ping now runs for the whole fault window.
#    It is strictly tighter than the poll in BOTH directions: it caught an outage the sampler
#    missed entirely (LIEWINDOW=0 beside LOSS=4/90), and it refused one the sampler
#    over-reported by a full poll interval (LOSS=12/90 beside SECS=17). One run lost
#    EXACTLY ONE PACKET — a single post-injection sample would almost certainly have called
#    that ABSORBED, reading as resilience.
NCS2_XRUNG_DECIDED_BY_PACKET_LOSS=confirmed

# ③ …and the cluster keeps TWO records of a node's address, maintained by different
#    components, which disagreed and stayed that way. Calico's `IPv4Address` annotation
#    followed the move to 10.77.0.22. The node's `InternalIP` — published by kubelet from the
#    `--node-ip` flag it was given at join time — kept reading 10.77.0.12 indefinitely.
#
#    The stale one is the one `kubectl get nodes -o wide` prints. Bug class #1, in the field
#    an operator looks at first.
NCS2_KUBELET_INTERNALIP_DOES_NOT_TRACK_A_MOVED_ADDRESS=confirmed

# ④ AND A CORRECTION TO A NATURAL EXTRAPOLATION. Appendix O measured `first-found` taking a
#    freshly-created dummy interface — HIGHER ifindex wins — in 20 s. `cidr=` does NOT order
#    its candidates that way: a dummy holding a matching address at a higher ifindex was
#    ignored for 205 s AND across a full calico-node restart, while the incumbent wire address
#    still matched. The cross-node F.6 row was designed twice because of it, and it is why
#    that row MOVES the address rather than offering a competing one.
NCS2_CIDR_METHOD_DOES_NOT_PREFER_HIGHER_IFINDEX=confirmed

# ── STILL not measured, and named rather than implied ───────────────────────────────────
# The tunnel deleted on the PEER rather than the leader (symmetric, cheap, not run); a
# partition of the wire itself; and anything needing three or more nodes, where half a fleet
# can be fine and say so. `tests/test-cross-node-chaos.sh` §4 prints the same list on every
# run, so a reader who never opens this file still learns what is uncovered.
NCS2_XROW_PEER_SIDE_TUNNEL_DELETE=not-yet-written
NCS2_XROW_WIRE_PARTITION=not-yet-written
NCS2_THREE_NODE_SCENARIOS=not-yet-written
